2:Ta40,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does 'Vedanta' literally mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vedanta means 'the end of the Vedas' (Veda + anta). It refers to the Upanishads, which form the final and philosophical portion of the Vedic canon, and the school of thought derived from them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the three schools of Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The three main schools are: Advaita Vedanta (non-dualism, taught by Adi Shankaracharya), Dvaita Vedanta (dualism, taught by Madhvacharya), and Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism, taught by Ramanujacharya)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Vedanta a religion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vedanta is a philosophy, not a religion. It provides the metaphysical framework underlying much of Hindu thought, but it can be studied and practiced independently of religious ritual."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the central teaching of Advaita Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The central teaching is 'Tat Tvam Asi' — That Thou Art. The individual soul (Atman) is not separate from the ultimate reality (Brahman). Liberation is the recognition of this identity."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is Vedanta different from yoga?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vedanta is primarily a philosophical system that answers 'what is real?' and 'who am I?' Yoga is a practical system of disciplines. Jnana Yoga, one of the four yoga paths, is most closely aligned with Vedanta."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a Westerner practice Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Vedanta is a system of self-inquiry and philosophical investigation. Swami Vivekananda's historic lectures in the West in 1893 demonstrated its universal relevance. It requires no conversion."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What texts are central to Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Prasthanatrayi — three canonical texts: the Upanishads (primary scripture), the Bhagavad Gita (scripture in dialogue form), and the Brahma Sutras (systematic treatise). All three schools of Vedanta have written commentaries on these."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the practical benefit of studying Vedanta?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vedanta provides a framework for understanding suffering, identity, and liberation. Practically, it cultivates dispassion (Vairagya), discrimination (Viveka), and equanimity — qualities that reduce reactivity and increase clarity."}}]}0:{"buildId":"3qmk8dgXQ5Jwwm3DDqgZ1","rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$2"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"What is Vedanta? A Complete Beginner's Guide\",\"description\":\"Vedanta is the philosophical crown of the Vedas, exploring the nature of ultimate reality (Brahman) and the self (Atman). A complete beginner's guide to Advaita, Dvaita, and Vishishtadvaita.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/what-is-vedanta\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/what-is-vedanta\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-03-04\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-03-04\",\"articleSection\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"keywords\":[\"what is vedanta\"],\"about\":[{\"@type\":\"Thing\",\"name\":\"what is vedanta\"}],\"isPartOf\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"name\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/ancient-wisdom-philosophies\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\"},\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com\"}}"}}],"$L3","$L4","$L5"]}],["$L6","$L7"],"$L8"]}],"loading":null,"isPartial":false}
